WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.000
Today we begin optics with mirrors.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.755
Before collecting formulas, let's ask what a mirror actually does to a ray of

00:00:08.755 --> 00:00:09.060
light.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.220
At the point where the ray hits, draw a normal: a line perpendicular to the

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.000
reflecting surface.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.040
The angle of incidence is measured between that normal and the incoming ray.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.280
The reflected ray makes the same angle with the normal, on the other side.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.320
These two rays and the normal lie in one plane.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.260
Simple rule, powerful consequences.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.780
Suppose a question says the incident ray makes sixty degrees with the mirror

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.220
surface.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:35.940
What is the angle of reflection?

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.320
Take a moment.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.140
It is thirty degrees, not sixty.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.540
The normal is perpendicular to the surface, so we subtract from ninety before

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:45.780
using the reflection law.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:47.720
Now picture a plane mirror.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.460
Reflected rays reaching your eyes diverge, but their backward extensions meet

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.140
behind the mirror.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.480
That meeting point is a virtual image.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.080
Light does not actually travel through that point behind an opaque mirror.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.900
For a stationary plane mirror, an object twenty centimeters in front produces

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.120
an upright, same size image twenty centimeters behind it.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.760
Object and image are forty centimeters apart.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:15.340
In a ray diagram, solid lines represent actual light paths; dashed lines show

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.100
backward extensions.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.780
This distinction will matter even more when we curve the mirror.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.460
Our first JEE habit is therefore: draw the normal, identify the angle, and

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.920
decide whether actual rays or their extensions meet.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.240
A concave mirror has its reflecting surface curved inward, facing our object.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.800
A convex mirror bulges toward the object.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.460
The pole is the middle of the mirror, and the principal axis joins the pole to

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.260
the sphere's center of curvature.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.160
Near the axis, parallel rays reflected by a concave mirror meet at its

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:49.920
principal focus.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.980
A convex mirror sends them apart; their backward extensions meet at a virtual

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.000
focus behind it.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.340
For spherical mirrors in the paraxial approximation, the focal length is half

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.540
the radius of curvature.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.540
Paraxial means rays close to the axis and making small angles with it.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.580
Farther out rays need not share exactly the same focus: that is spherical

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.200
aberration.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.700
Now let's prevent the classic sign convention mix up.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.160
Throughout this lesson, incident light travels left to right.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.300
Put the pole at zero, and take rightward distances as positive.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.780
Every distance is measured from the pole.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.160
A real object on the left has negative object distance, u.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.340
A concave mirror's focus is on the left, so f is negative.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.040
A convex mirror's focus is on the right, so f is positive.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.660
Image distance, v, may be negative or positive depending on its location.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.620
A concave radius of negative twenty centimeters gives f equal to negative ten

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.080
centimeters.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.840
Convex radius positive twenty gives positive ten.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.160
Some references use a different convention.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.340
Their physics is the same, but do not borrow their signs while keeping our

00:02:59.340 --> 00:02:59.660
equation.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.620
Here we use one over f equals one over v plus one over u, consistently.

00:03:05.856 --> 00:03:08.856
Let's build a real image rather than just name it.

00:03:09.296 --> 00:03:12.236
Our concave mirror has focal length negative ten centimeters.

00:03:13.116 --> 00:03:16.216
Place a small upright object thirty centimeters to its left.

00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:20.076
Draw one ray from the object's tip parallel to the axis.

00:03:21.016 --> 00:03:23.336
After reflection, it passes through the focus.

00:03:24.456 --> 00:03:25.716
Draw a second ray to the pole.

00:03:26.536 --> 00:03:31.076
At the pole the normal lies along the axis, so equal angles give its reflected

00:03:31.076 --> 00:03:31.556
direction.

00:03:32.036 --> 00:03:35.936
Where those two reflected rays actually meet, we locate the image tip.

00:03:36.636 --> 00:03:40.640
Our diagram is a paraxial schematic, so the mirror is treated close to its

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:40.856
pole

00:03:40.856 --> 00:03:42.016
for the ray construction.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:44.456
Now calculate.

00:03:45.416 --> 00:03:47.156
Object distance is negative thirty centimeters.

00:03:47.976 --> 00:03:52.136
One over image distance equals one over focal length minus one over object

00:03:52.136 --> 00:03:52.556
distance.

00:03:52.936 --> 00:03:57.456
That is negative one tenth plus one thirtieth, giving negative one fifteenth

00:03:57.456 --> 00:03:58.156
per centimeter.

00:03:58.996 --> 00:04:01.076
Therefore v is negative fifteen centimeters.

00:04:01.556 --> 00:04:05.562
The image lies fifteen centimeters to the left of the mirror, between the

00:04:05.562 --> 00:04:05.836
focus

00:04:05.836 --> 00:04:07.076
and center of curvature.

00:04:07.836 --> 00:04:10.716
It is real because reflected rays actually converge there.

00:04:11.456 --> 00:04:12.236
What about its size?

00:04:12.996 --> 00:04:15.656
Mirror magnification is negative v divided by u.

00:04:16.636 --> 00:04:19.256
Substituting both signed distances gives negative one half.

00:04:19.896 --> 00:04:23.276
The minus sign says inverted; the magnitude says half as tall.

00:04:24.116 --> 00:04:27.636
A two centimeter object therefore has signed image height negative one

00:04:27.636 --> 00:04:27.976
centimeter.

00:04:28.896 --> 00:04:32.596
Notice how three checks agree: the ray intersection, the calculated position,

00:04:33.036 --> 00:04:34.156
and the predicted orientation.

00:04:35.076 --> 00:04:39.096
In JEE numerical questions, write signs before arithmetic, keep units

00:04:39.096 --> 00:04:40.936
consistent, then describe the image.

00:04:41.536 --> 00:04:44.849
A correct number with the wrong real or virtual interpretation is an

00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:45.336
unfinished

00:04:45.336 --> 00:04:45.696
answer.

00:04:45.912 --> 00:04:50.564
Keep the same concave mirror, but move our object to five centimeters in

00:04:50.564 --> 00:04:50.952
front.

00:04:51.612 --> 00:04:53.752
That puts it between the focus and the pole.

00:04:54.432 --> 00:04:56.532
Do you expect a smaller real image again?

00:04:57.432 --> 00:04:58.452
Pause and look at the rays.

00:04:58.872 --> 00:05:00.872
Now the reflected rays spread apart.

00:05:01.512 --> 00:05:04.952
Their backward extensions meet behind the mirror, above the axis.

00:05:05.292 --> 00:05:08.272
We get a virtual, upright, enlarged image.

00:05:08.272 --> 00:05:11.152
Let's make the calculation explain the picture.

00:05:11.952 --> 00:05:15.452
One over v is negative one tenth minus negative one fifth.

00:05:15.952 --> 00:05:20.232
That becomes positive one tenth per centimeter, so v is positive ten

00:05:20.232 --> 00:05:21.572
centimeters.

00:05:21.572 --> 00:05:25.052
Positive image distance means behind this mirror in our coordinate system.

00:05:25.652 --> 00:05:30.032
Magnification is negative ten divided by negative five, which equals positive

00:05:30.032 --> 00:05:30.412
two.

00:05:30.892 --> 00:05:33.172
The image is twice as tall and upright.

00:05:33.652 --> 00:05:36.032
A virtual image is not automatically smaller.

00:05:36.452 --> 00:05:38.252
That shortcut would fail right here.

00:05:38.972 --> 00:05:43.212
As a useful map for a real object and a concave mirror: beyond the center, the

00:05:43.212 --> 00:05:47.636
image is real and smaller; at the center, it is real and the same size;

00:05:47.636 --> 00:05:48.072
between

00:05:48.072 --> 00:05:50.392
center and focus, it is real and larger.

00:05:51.312 --> 00:05:53.692
Inside the focus, it becomes virtual and larger.

00:05:54.392 --> 00:05:57.732
At the focus itself, reflected paraxial rays are parallel.

00:05:58.332 --> 00:06:02.432
There is no finite image distance; we describe the image as at infinity.

00:06:03.192 --> 00:06:05.812
Do not divide by zero and report an ordinary number.

00:06:06.412 --> 00:06:08.852
Our two worked examples straddle that focal boundary.

00:06:09.552 --> 00:06:13.412
In a JEE problem, first locate the object relative to the focus.

00:06:14.012 --> 00:06:17.212
That prediction gives you a quick way to catch an algebraic sign error.

00:06:17.424 --> 00:06:21.924
Now switch to a convex mirror with focal length positive fifteen centimeters.

00:06:22.764 --> 00:06:27.124
Our real object is thirty centimeters in front, so u remains negative thirty.

00:06:27.844 --> 00:06:32.004
One over v equals one fifteenth minus negative one thirtieth.

00:06:32.004 --> 00:06:33.504
That is one tenth per centimeter.

00:06:34.484 --> 00:06:38.013
Image distance is positive ten centimeters, behind the mirror, between the

00:06:38.013 --> 00:06:38.204
pole

00:06:38.364 --> 00:06:39.364
and the virtual focus.

00:06:40.304 --> 00:06:44.264
Magnification is negative ten divided by negative thirty, giving positive one

00:06:44.264 --> 00:06:44.424
third.

00:06:45.164 --> 00:06:47.584
So the image is virtual, upright, and diminished.

00:06:48.324 --> 00:06:51.724
Try one more in your head: the same mirror, but a real object fifteen

00:06:51.724 --> 00:06:52.464
centimeters away.

00:06:53.104 --> 00:06:54.044
What is the image distance?

00:06:54.684 --> 00:06:55.444
Give yourself a moment.

00:06:56.104 --> 00:07:00.384
One over v becomes one fifteenth plus one fifteenth, so v is positive seven

00:07:00.384 --> 00:07:01.244
point five centimeters.

00:07:02.564 --> 00:07:04.064
Magnification is positive one half.

00:07:04.584 --> 00:07:06.404
It is still behind the mirror and smaller.

00:07:07.184 --> 00:07:11.204
This explains why convex mirrors can show a wider scene while shrinking the

00:07:11.204 --> 00:07:11.584
objects in it.

00:07:12.284 --> 00:07:16.304
For a real object in front of an ordinary convex mirror, the virtual, upright,

00:07:16.724 --> 00:07:17.744
diminished pattern is dependable.

00:07:18.244 --> 00:07:22.784
The words real object are essential: a virtual object made by another optical

00:07:22.784 --> 00:07:24.404
element needs separate analysis.

00:07:25.524 --> 00:07:26.984
Finally, our sign check routine.

00:07:27.784 --> 00:07:29.284
Fix the pole and positive direction.

00:07:30.004 --> 00:07:31.264
Write signed u and f.

00:07:31.824 --> 00:07:32.484
Solve for v.

00:07:33.324 --> 00:07:34.524
Calculate negative v over u.

00:07:35.084 --> 00:07:39.324
Then interpret position, orientation, and size, and check them against a ray

00:07:39.324 --> 00:07:39.584
sketch.

00:07:40.324 --> 00:07:44.224
For a plane mirror, the same convention gives v equal to negative u and

00:07:44.224 --> 00:07:45.644
magnification positive one.

00:07:46.384 --> 00:07:49.824
Keep this method consistent and mirror questions stop feeling like a guessing

00:07:49.824 --> 00:07:50.064
game.
